--TEST--
`Core\Sse::current` is refused in a program that is not writing an event stream and answers a handle in one that is, the call that opens the stream being the bound — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate` and `rule:errors/ambiguous-input-refused`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Before anything opened one this is an ordinary program, which is every
// command-line program and every `spawn script` child as well. The refusal is a
// `LogicError` a program catches and names the member it called, because what
// was wrong is the call and not the program: there is no arrangement under which
// asking a program with no stream for its stream is the right thing to do.
var $before = "";
try {
    Core\Sse::current();
} catch (LogicError $refused) {
    $before = $refused->message;
}
echo $before, "\n";

// After, the same program answers, and the events reach the stream rather than
// a second one — off a connection there is no cell to open, so they arrive in
// this program's own output.
var $after = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    Core\Sse::stream();
    Core\Sse::current()->send("open");
});
echo $after;
--EXPECT--
`Core\Sse::current` needs a program writing an event stream and this is not one: a stream is opened by `Core\Sse::stream` for the request that answers with it and by `Core\Sse::upgrade` for the isolate that outlives it, and a response writing an ordinary body over time is neither
data: open

